# Review Notes

## Summary
T062: externally recorded traces are now analyzable. `oal friction`
accepts serve-session directories (trace.jsonl plus
capability-report.json, gated on `sessions: "allow"`), and `oal trace
import <har> --contract <spec>` normalizes a HAR 1.2 into trace-event.v1
with gateway routing and validation. Friction gained origin "external".

## Review round
First implementation verdicts were fix_first/land with two blockers and
a security gap. All were fixed in three follow-up commits (cf2e563,
f85e3a6, 0e0058d) before landing:

- Blocker: gateway validators emit pointer "" for root-level body
  violations; the import now maps empty pointers to "/" so written
  events satisfy trace-event.v1.
- Blocker: HAR status 0 (Chrome failed requests) wrote an invalid
  status; the import now records response null, validation reads
  not_evaluated, and a truthful OAL-TRACE-IMPORT-RESPONSE-ABSENT fires.
- Security: the Redactor had an empty secret registry, so credential
  values repeated in text bodies, malformed-JSON bodies, query strings,
  and paths survived verbatim. The import now collects sensitive-named
  header values as run secrets before writing anything; docs state the
  exact registry behavior.
- Stdin import ("-") now works as the help text promises.
- Undeclared Content-Type reports media_type_unsupported (and the
  media_type_rejected friction incident) instead of a false missing-body
  violation; base64 HAR content digests as the decoded bytes;
  credential_present accepts every sensitive header name, mirroring the
  runner recorder.
- detectGeneratedHandleReuse skips external trials: the recorded
  service issued the handle, so reuse is the protocol working, not mock
  fidelity.

